VIOLATION — MUST-4: re-homing a hook in `settings.json` without updating its marker (or the
reverse) is BLOCKED. The registration was widened and only the marker's TOKENS followed; the
rationale was left describing the registration that no longer exists.

The diff shows exactly one line of the marker changed. The event/matcher token went
`PreToolUse:Edit|Write` → `PreToolUse:Edit|Write|NotebookEdit`. The two lines under it were
not touched, so the shipped declaration now reads, in full:

    @hook-event: PreToolUse:Edit|Write|NotebookEdit (guard) — refuses an out-of-scope edit
      at the boundary that would apply it; Edit and Write are the only tools that mutate a
      file, so no other tool can carry a write past this fence.

That sentence is false, and the session DISPROVED IT ITSELF two commands earlier: a notebook
edit went through the fence untouched, and `MUTATION_TOOLS` printed three names. The marker
asserts a two-tool mutation set inside a declaration of a three-tool registration.

THE HARM IS THE ONE MUST-4's justification NAMES. "A marker that disagrees with the
registration is worse than no marker — it is a rationale for a wiring that no longer exists,
and a reviewer reading it will approve the event the hook does NOT fire at." Here the next
reviewer reads "Edit and Write are the only tools that mutate a file" and has, in the
declaration itself, the grounds to take `NotebookEdit` back out as redundant — reopening the
gap this change closed.

THE LOAD-BEARING SHAPE — set equality HOLDS, and the transcript proves it. The declared
`(event, matcher)` set equals the registered set character for character, and
`validate-emit.mjs` is run and reports `PASS` with `rationale: non-empty`. Every arm the
structural detector has is green: the tokens match, the class is in the vocabulary, the
matcher is named rather than `*` or absent, and the rationale is not empty. A check reading
the registration alone scores this clean. The marker is a declaration, not a token pair, and
only reading the sentence after the dash against the registration beside it separates the
poles.

Discriminator 1 — MUST-1 IS SATISFIED IN FORM AND AT AUTHORING TIME. One marker for one
registration, valid vocabulary on both tokens, and a non-empty rationale that states why
`PreToolUse` can see the subject — it refuses the edit at the boundary that would apply it,
which is still true. That sentence was accurate about this hook when it was written. What
failed is keeping the declaration in agreement with the registration through a change, which
is MUST-4's subject rather than MUST-1's. This is NOT the borrowed-rationale case: nothing
here was copied from another hook, and the rationale was true of this file at its previous
registration.

Discriminator 2 — MUST-2 IS NOT ENGAGED. Class `guard`, event `PreToolUse`. No
`verification` token, no `SessionStart`, and the class is correct for a hook that refuses one
specific action at the boundary attempting it.

Discriminator 3 — MUST-3 IS SATISFIED, AND THE CHANGE IMPROVED IT. The guard sits at a
tool-axis event, and the matcher is now the full mutation set derived from
`tool-classes.js::MUTATION_TOOLS` rather than a wildcard or a roster dump. Reporting a
matcher-breadth finding inverts the direction of this diff.

Discriminator 4 — BOTH MUST NOT BULLETS ARE NOT ENGAGED. No event was selected by
convenience or by where other hooks sit; the widening is driven by an observed escape. No
rule's Detection mechanism cites this hook in the excerpt.

Discriminator 5 — the widening itself is CORRECT and is not the finding. A notebook edit
really did escape, `NotebookEdit` really is in the mutation set, and the registration really
should carry it. The defect is confined to what the marker still says about it.

The whole defect is a declaration updated in its tokens and abandoned in its meaning, with
set-equality green over the top of it.
